nodecode-enhance-prompt is a rewrite of tuancookiez-hub's tuancookiez-hub/hermes-enhance-prompt:

  https://github.com/tuancookiez-hub/hermes-enhance-prompt
  commit 8417827ff80db2335e18742c1f5317aeb4b036cb
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 tuancookiez-hub (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the rewrite itself - the system prompt and the user-message template
from prompts.py, character for character, and the three stage hints from the
Desktop half's plugin.js (clarify, detail, horizon), which are what makes a
second click a different transformation rather than a longer text. With them:
the `enhance_prompt` tool and its arguments (the draft, a stage, the eight
character floor), the `/enhance` command, and strip_wrappers - the thinking
blocks, the fullwidth channel tags, the code fence and the outer quotes a
serving backend puts around an answer, stripped in the same order. The Python
is Common Lisp here: one side call, NLE:COMPLETE, in place of ctx.llm.complete
and the Desktop's llm.oneshot, and a draft effect in place of host.composer, so
the command writes its answer back into the shell's own composer where the
runtime offers that: the command is registered as reading the draft when it
can be, and takes the draft as its argument everywhere else.

Left out: the Desktop chrome, which a cell cannot draw - the sparkle button
beside Send, the before/after score it prints, the click-while-spinning cancel,
the keybinding, and the backup draft the button held. The score was a second
model call per click, so it is gone with the button; `/enhance undo` is the
discard it replaced, kept per chat in memory rather than in a component. Also
left out: the plugin's own catalogue and validation files, which are Hermes's
packaging rather than the plugin's behaviour.
